#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087573 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087757 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8cc1de8-5ac0-5607-a2eb-6eec375721e8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Argument of a Complex Number in the First Quadrant"}] :question/id #uuid "a930b12e-d598-584e-82cc-63efb6c91e25" :question/math-academy-id "e-5773" :question/problem "Find, in radians to two decimal places, the argument of $z=2+7\\textrm{i}$." :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/da/da91da7425e6a02a202881eb1db3f1049d3f9cd204c0c1793c30e2d95676783b.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the first quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore\n\n$$\n0< \\arg(z)< \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nFurther,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\arg (z) &= \\arctan (\\frac{7}{2}) \\approx 1.29\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087574 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087758 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "833ac63c-235b-5059-ab7c-56eb1e44d79e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Argument of a Complex Number in the Second Quadrant"}] :question/id #uuid "591a8f2f-d107-50f8-9803-3f3a85185653" :question/math-academy-id "e-5774" :question/problem "Find, in radians, the argument of $z=-1+\\textrm{i}\\sqrt{3}$." :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/1b/1bd35c5350e1dc1988cf98b8598a350701ef0b2a02a053b97ae2c89726de50c6.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the second quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}< \\arg(z)< \\pi\n$$\n\nIf $\\alpha$ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan α &= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{1} \\\\\nα &= \\arctan (\\sqrt{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\arg(z) = \\pi - \\dfrac\\pi 3 = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087575 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087759 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "54588722-bb80-54ca-b100-03aca39e0c17" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Argument of a Complex Number in the Fourth Quadrant"}] :question/id #uuid "4b989810-55cb-5825-b4d2-9b05e03d6eec" :question/math-academy-id "e-5775" :question/problem "Find, in radians, the argument of $z=1-\\textrm{i}$." :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/5b/5b9ec84d8c3176c85c1b5ea546fc138231a129a11b906d786578616a1dd16cb1.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}< \\arg(z)< 2\\pi\n$$\n\nIf $\\alpha$ is the angle that $z$ makes with the positive real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\alpha = \\arctan\\left(1\\right) = \\dfrac\\pi 4\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\arg(z) = 2\\pi - \\dfrac\\pi 4 =\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087589 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087769 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a560db4d-e9d3-5d89-b2cd-c886f32db9df" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Argument of a Complex Number in the Third Quadrant"}] :question/id #uuid "d1a9b27b-80e7-53af-add8-4c740d750746" :question/math-academy-id "e-6224" :question/problem "Find, in radians to two decimal places, the argument of $z=-\\sqrt{3}-\\textrm{i}$." :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/00/0095750158d9a296c9950c2d753ffe820735298c96cdf4de23aef0747862658d.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the third quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\pi< \\arg(z)< \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nIf $\\alpha$ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nα &= \\arctan (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) = \\frac{π}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\arg(z) = \\pi +\\dfrac\\pi 6 = \\dfrac{7\\pi}{6}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233710 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087758 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "833ac63c-235b-5059-ab7c-56eb1e44d79e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Argument of a Complex Number in the Second Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b353bd1b-1c5e-529b-86dd-9fd5edfe9c4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b353bd1b-1c5e-529b-86dd-9fd5edfe9c4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232406} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a8a3f0f-e235-523e-966e-179bafec3a3b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232405}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2ca890be-c883-5ea3-81cb-a98892d9c262" :question/math-academy-id "q-275157" :question/problem "Find, **in radians**, the argument of $z=-4+6\\text{i}.$ Round your final answer to **two decimal places**.\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$ \n\n$\\operatorname{arg}(z)\\approx $\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/32/325444dbc509efd27f6c5bf16c751b137532e2566a9dfaa69f92d9ce16bac51b.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the second quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname{arg}(z)<\\pi .$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}\\alpha  & =\\frac{6}{4} \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\operatorname{arctan}(\\frac{6}{4}) \\\\  & =0.982\\text{ }793…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}(z)=\\pi -0.982\\text{ }793…\\approx 2.16,\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299780 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087759 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "54588722-bb80-54ca-b100-03aca39e0c17" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Argument of a Complex Number in the Fourth Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1ed74be-5bd1-5cfb-83ae-8da1c46abddc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298461}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1ed74be-5bd1-5cfb-83ae-8da1c46abddc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298461} :answer-field/id #uuid "664bc144-8adc-5276-97ef-e29bdce72ee9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298983}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e5b42c0b-e569-59e6-b7d2-a632bdfada48" :question/math-academy-id "q-319581" :question/problem "Find, **in radians**, the **exact value** of the argument of $z=8\\sqrt{3}-8\\text{i}.$\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$ \n\n$arg⁡(z)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/78/78399e67e0ba69c1d7848a41aa5d4a5f8040fc578aab30a99f7e19ac86ece179.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}<arg⁡(z)<2\\pi .$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the positive real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡\\alpha  & =\\frac{8}{8\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\alpha  & =arctan\\,(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =arg⁡(z)=2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351436 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087758 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "833ac63c-235b-5059-ab7c-56eb1e44d79e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Argument of a Complex Number in the Second Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3df27b3c-a1ee-528a-81f0-7eb8a3074c26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349009} {:answer/id #uuid "4511a7fb-9e8b-585c-bf7a-b508f7bc5005" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349074} {:answer/id #uuid "683bf0ce-269f-5e2a-bfe2-1df524011252" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.63" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349405} {:answer/id #uuid "b3a9b871-596b-5ff9-86da-0a8d53cc18e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350038} {:answer/id #uuid "e2e11477-ce5a-5ebd-ae7c-aa494d89f849" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350441}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4511a7fb-9e8b-585c-bf7a-b508f7bc5005" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349074} :answer-field/id #uuid "21d06d80-2d3e-51a4-8fe9-e63d356883e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350769}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5b6d5c20-9bf0-5f51-a0b6-6ea0521fb716" :question/math-academy-id "q-8257" :question/problem "Find, in radians to two decimal places, the argument of $z=-3+4\\text{i}.$ \n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/37/373ea8254a9816f67bfbcaf993348f409c1047284d09c43e5d41e68b15c5af58.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the second quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<arg⁡(z)<\\pi .$ If $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡\\alpha  & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\alpha  & =arctan⁡(\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & ≈0.927\\,30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\theta =arg⁡(z)≈\\pi -0.927\\,30≈2.21\n$$\n\nto two decimal places."}]}
